import{_ as t,o,c as a,a2 as s}from"./chunks/framework.CS-cBh1g.js";const p=JSON.parse('{"title":"How agents work tasks","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"agents/tasks.md","filePath":"agents/tasks.md"}'),n={name:"agents/tasks.md"};function r(h,e,i,d,c,l){return o(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[s('<h1 id="how-agents-work-tasks" tabindex="-1">How agents work tasks <a class="header-anchor" href="#how-agents-work-tasks" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;How agents work tasks&quot;">​</a></h1><p>This is the agent&#39;s side of the <a href="/Mayhem/suite/tasks">Tasks</a> board: what it reads, what it writes, and when it waits for a person. Agents connected with the prompt from <strong>Connect an agent</strong> follow it automatically. It&#39;s documented here so you know what to expect, and so you can check an agent is doing it right.</p><h2 id="where-tasks-live" tabindex="-1">Where tasks live <a class="header-anchor" href="#where-tasks-live" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Where tasks live&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Each task is one JSON file in the robot repo: <code>tools/mayhem/review/&lt;id&gt;.json</code>. Feedback from anywhere in the app lands there with its context, so the agent knows exactly what you meant:</p><ul><li>a <strong>log moment</strong>: the recording, seconds into it, the code revision, the field pose;</li><li>an <strong>auto step</strong>: the routine and step;</li><li>a <strong>state machine</strong>: the machine, state or transition;</li><li>anything else right-clicked: the page, panel and item.</li></ul><p>Commit task files with the code (or at least push them) so teammates and agents on other computers see them.</p><h2 id="the-loop" tabindex="-1">The loop <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-loop" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The loop&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>Agent does</th><th>Board column</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td><code>list_tasks</code>, then <code>get_task</code> for the context. Opens the linked log with the log tools.</td><td>New</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td><code>claim_task</code>. Mayhem records the current commit as the task&#39;s base.</td><td>Agent planning</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td><code>propose_plan</code> in plain English: what will change on the robot and how you&#39;ll see it. <strong>Then waits.</strong> No code changes before approval.</td><td>Plan to approve</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>After you approve: changes the code, commits with a <code>Mayhem-Task: &lt;id&gt;</code> line, calls <code>report_progress</code>, then runs the sim proof for the task (<code>run_scenarios</code> with <code>task</code>).</td><td>Agent working</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td><code>finish_task</code>.</td><td>Ready to check</td></tr></tbody></table><p>If you send a plan or a change back, your note goes into the task and it returns to the agent. Your note is what it reads to try again, which is why sending back needs one.</p><h2 id="questions-happen-in-the-terminal" tabindex="-1">Questions happen in the terminal <a class="header-anchor" href="#questions-happen-in-the-terminal" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Questions happen in the terminal&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The agent runs in your terminal, and that is where it talks to you. When feedback leaves out something only a person knows (which button, which match, what &quot;slower&quot; means), it asks there and stops; your answer goes into the plan, so whoever approves the plan sees what it rests on. Tasks have no question thread.</p><h2 id="the-sim-proof-is-recorded-not-described" tabindex="-1">The sim proof is recorded, not described <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-sim-proof-is-recorded-not-described" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The sim proof is recorded, not described&quot;">​</a></h2><p>When the agent runs the sim proof for a task, the tool compares the task&#39;s base with its newest commit and writes the result onto that commit in the task file: how many scenarios ran, which failed, which behave differently, who ran it and when. That record is what you see on the task in <strong>Ready to check</strong>. An agent can&#39;t type it in, and a newer commit has none until a proof is run on it. If the agent finished without one, the task says <strong>Sim proof not run on this commit</strong> and you can run it with one click.</p><h2 id="after-you-accept" tabindex="-1">After you accept <a class="header-anchor" href="#after-you-accept" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;After you accept&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The agent opens a pull request and keeps the <code>Mayhem-Task: &lt;id&gt;</code> line in its commit message. From there the app follows the change by itself, and the agent does nothing more: <strong>in main</strong> once the pull request is merged (a squash merge counts), <strong>on the robot</strong> once someone deploys and checks the robot, <strong>in a log</strong> once a log recorded on that code is collected. Agents never deploy.</p><h2 id="code-simulate-read-fix" tabindex="-1">Code, simulate, read, fix <a class="header-anchor" href="#code-simulate-read-fix" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Code, simulate, read, fix&quot;">​</a></h2><p>For a behavior bug, agents are expected to close the loop before asking you:</p><ol><li>Change the code, and regenerate <code>MayhemProject.h</code> with <code>tools/mayhem/export.sh</code> if the project or <code>machines.json</code> changed.</li><li>Run the relevant <a href="/Mayhem/robot-code/scenarios">sim proof scenarios</a> on the uncommitted code (<code>run_scenarios</code>).</li><li>Read the result&#39;s log: <code>log_states</code> for a state machine problem; <code>log_anomalies</code>, <code>log_what_changed</code> and <code>log_table</code> for anything else.</li><li>Fix and repeat. Before handing off, add a scenario that reproduces the bug, so it stays fixed, then commit and run everything on the commit against the base (step 4 of the loop).</li></ol><h2 id="what-gets-accepted-without-you" tabindex="-1">What gets accepted without you <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-gets-accepted-without-you" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What gets accepted without you&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Mayhem rates every reported commit with the same review the <a href="/Mayhem/suite/changes">Changes</a> page shows:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Risk</th><th>Means</th><th>Accepted automatically?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Red</strong></td><td>CAN IDs, current limits, brake/coast, motor directions, gear ratios, offsets; or generated code out of date</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Yellow</strong></td><td>Driver controls, state machines, other values, any other robot code, a loosened scenario</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Green</strong></td><td>Only auto paths changed, and every changed path still finishes</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td><strong>None</strong></td><td>Nothing that reaches the robot changed (notes, a new scenario)</td><td>Yes</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Most real fixes touch C++ logic that Mayhem can&#39;t read, so they&#39;re yellow and wait for a person. Judge those by the sim proof.</p><h2 id="stalled-agents" tabindex="-1">Stalled agents <a class="header-anchor" href="#stalled-agents" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Stalled agents&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A task an agent has held for a day without an update shows <strong>no update for …</strong>. Use <strong>Back to New</strong> to take it back; another agent (or the same one, restarted) can pick it up.</p>',24)])])}const m=t(n,[["render",r]]);export{p as __pageData,m as default};
